# Quản lý Hệ thống Tiêm phòng - Java Spring Boot Development Rules

## Project Information
- Project Name: Vaccination Management System (Quản lý Hệ thống Tiêm phòng)
- Type: SE358 Group Project - Software Engineering Project Management
- Course: SE358 - Software Development Project Management
- Repository: https://github.com/thiengl2015/Quan_ly_he_thong_tiem_phong

## Java & Spring Boot Stack
- Java Version: 21 LTS (latest)
- Spring Boot: 3.4.x (latest stable)
- Build Tool: Maven (pom.xml)
- IDE: Cursor IDE with this .cursorrules

## Database & ORM
- Database: MySQL 8.0+
- ORM: Spring Data JPA with Hibernate
- Migrations: Flyway for schema management
- Naming: snake_case for tables (user_profiles, vaccination_schedules)

## Project Structure
```
src/main/java/com/example/vaccination/
  ├── config/           (Spring configuration, Security config)
  ├── controller/       (REST API endpoints)
  ├── service/          (Business logic, vaccination service)
  ├── repository/       (Spring Data JPA repositories)
  ├── model/entity/     (JPA entities - User, Vaccination, Schedule)
  ├── dto/              (Data Transfer Objects - request/response)
  ├── exception/        (Custom exceptions, error handling)
  ├── security/         (JWT, authentication, authorization)
  ├── util/             (Utility classes, helpers)
  └── Application.java  (Main Spring Boot application)

src/main/resources/
  ├── application.properties
  ├── application-dev.properties
  ├── application-prod.properties
  ├── db/migration/     (Flyway SQL migration files)
  └── messages.properties (i18n messages)

src/test/java/
  └── (mirror structure of src/main/java with Test suffix)
```

## Code Standards & Conventions

### Naming Conventions
1. Classes: PascalCase (UserController, VaccinationService)
2. Methods/Variables: camelCase (getUserById, vaccinationList)
3. Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (MAX_RETRIES, DEFAULT_TIMEOUT)
4. Database Tables: snake_case (user_profiles, vaccination_records)
5. Database Columns: snake_case (user_id, created_at, updated_at)
6. Packages: lowercase with dots (com.example.vaccination.controller)

### Class & Method Standards
1. Use meaningful, descriptive names - avoid abbreviations
2. PascalCase for class names
3. camelCase for method names
4. Keep methods focused and readable (max 30 lines)
5. Use final keyword for immutable fields and parameters
6. Use generics, avoid raw types
7. Private fields, public getters/setters or @Getter @Setter
8. Add @Slf4j or Logger for all classes that log

### Annotations & Imports
1. Use @Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller annotations correctly
2. Prefer constructor injection over @Autowired
3. Use @Configuration for bean definitions
4. Use @Value for simple properties, @ConfigurationProperties for complex
5. Don't use wildcard imports (java.util.*)
6. Organize imports: java → javax → spring → custom packages

## Spring Boot Best Practices

### Application Configuration
1. Use application.properties or application.yml (NOT both)
2. Use profiles: application-dev.properties, application-prod.properties
3. Never commit secrets - use environment variables or .env files
4. Use @ConfigurationProperties for complex configurations
5. Example properties:
   ```
   spring.application.name=vaccination-system
   spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate
   spring.jpa.show-sql=false
   spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size=20
   logging.level.com.example.vaccination=DEBUG
   ```

### Spring Data JPA & Hibernate
1. Create entities in model/entity/ package
2. Use @Entity, @Table, @Id, @GeneratedValue annotations
3. Use @Column with proper mapping (nullable, unique, length)
4. Use @ManyToOne, @OneToMany, @ManyToMany for relationships
5. Use @Lazy for lazy-loaded relationships
6. Implement Serializable for entities
7. Use Spring Data JPA repositories (extends JpaRepository<T, ID>)
8. Create custom @Query methods only when necessary
9. Use @Transactional for operations requiring transactions
10. Use @Transactional(readOnly=true) for read operations

### REST API Design (Controllers)
1. Use @RestController for REST endpoints
2. Use @RequestMapping for base path, specific methods for actions
3. Use @GetMapping, @PostMapping, @PutMapping, @DeleteMapping
4. Return ResponseEntity<T> for proper HTTP status codes
5. Use @PathVariable for URL parameters
6. Use @RequestParam for query parameters
7. Use @RequestBody with @Valid for request validation
8. Create separate DTO classes for requests/responses
9. Example endpoints:
   ```
   GET    /api/vaccinations              (list all)
   GET    /api/vaccinations/{id}         (get one)
   POST   /api/vaccinations              (create)
   PUT    /api/vaccinations/{id}         (update)
   DELETE /api/vaccinations/{id}         (delete)
   ```

### Services & Business Logic
1. Put business logic in @Service classes
2. Name services with Service suffix (VaccinationService, UserService)
3. Use @Transactional for multi-step operations
4. Throw custom exceptions for business errors
5. Keep services focused on one domain

### Exception Handling
1. Create custom exceptions extending RuntimeException
2. Use @ControllerAdvice for global exception handling
3. Use @ExceptionHandler for specific exceptions
4. Return appropriate HTTP status codes:
   - 400 Bad Request (validation error)
   - 401 Unauthorized (auth failure)
   - 403 Forbidden (permission denied)
   - 404 Not Found (resource not found)
   - 500 Internal Server Error (unexpected error)
5. Log exceptions with stack trace at ERROR level
6. Don't expose internal exceptions to client
7. Example custom exception:
   ```java
   public class VaccinationNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {
     public VaccinationNotFoundException(Long id) {
       super("Vaccination not found with id: " + id);
     }
   }
   ```

### Logging
1. Use SLF4J with Logback (built-in with Spring Boot)
2. Use @Slf4j annotation from Lombok or Logger.getLogger()
3. Log levels: DEBUG (dev details), INFO (important), WARN (potential issues), ERROR (failures)
4. Don't log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, personal info)
5. Use structured logging with parameters
6. Example:
   ```java
   log.debug("Processing vaccination for user id: {}", userId);
   log.info("Vaccination created successfully with id: {}", vaccination.getId());
   log.error("Failed to send SMS reminder", exception);
   ```

## Security & Authentication

### Spring Security
1. Use Spring Security for authentication/authorization
2. Implement JWT token-based authentication for stateless APIs
3. Use @EnableWebSecurity for security configuration
4. Configure SecurityFilterChain bean
5. Use BCryptPasswordEncoder for password hashing
6. Never store plain text passwords

### JWT Authentication
1. Generate JWT tokens on successful login
2. Validate JWT tokens on each request
3. Use JWT filters for authentication
4. Token expiration: 1-24 hours depending on use case
5. Include user roles/authorities in JWT claims
6. Don't store sensitive data in JWT payload (it's encoded, not encrypted)

### Method-Level Security
1. Use @PreAuthorize for method access control
2. Use @Secured for role-based access
3. Use @PostAuthorize for return value filtering
4. Example:
   ```java
   @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
   public void deleteVaccination(Long id) { }
   
   @PreAuthorize("hasAuthority('EDIT_VACCINATION')")
   public Vaccination updateVaccination(Vaccination vac) { }
   ```

### Input Validation
1. Use @Valid with @NotNull, @NotBlank, @Email, @Min, @Max
2. Validate all user inputs in DTOs
3. Use custom validators for complex validation
4. Return 400 Bad Request for validation errors
5. Example DTO:
   ```java
   @Data
   public class VaccinationDTO {
     @NotNull(message = "User ID is required")
     private Long userId;
     
     @NotBlank(message = "Vaccine name is required")
     private String vaccineName;
     
     @Email(message = "Email should be valid")
     private String email;
   }
   ```

## Testing

### Unit Tests (JUnit 5 + Mockito)
1. Use JUnit 5 (Jupiter) for unit tests
2. Use Mockito for mocking dependencies
3. Test class naming: <Class>Test (e.g., VaccinationServiceTest)
4. Test method naming: test<MethodName><Condition><ExpectedResult>
5. Use @Mock for dependencies, @InjectMocks for class under test
6. Example:
   ```java
   @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
   class VaccinationServiceTest {
     @Mock
     private VaccinationRepository vaccinationRepository;
     
     @InjectMocks
     private VaccinationService vaccinationService;
     
     @Test
     void testGetVaccinationByIdWhenExistsThenReturnVaccination() {
       Long id = 1L;
       Vaccination vaccination = new Vaccination();
       when(vaccinationRepository.findById(id)).thenReturn(Optional.of(vaccination));
       
       Vaccination result = vaccinationService.getById(id);
       
       assertNotNull(result);
       assertEquals(id, result.getId());
     }
   }
   ```

### Integration Tests
1. Use @SpringBootTest for integration tests
2. Use @WebMvcTest for controller testing
3. Use @DataJpaTest for repository testing
4. Use @MockMvc for testing REST endpoints
5. Mock external services (email, SMS, external APIs)
6. Use test containers for real database testing if needed

### Test Coverage
1. Aim for 80%+ code coverage
2. Test both happy path and error scenarios
3. Test edge cases and boundary conditions
4. Write tests for public methods and important logic
5. Don't test getters/setters unless custom logic

## Database & Migrations (Flyway)

### Migration Files
1. Location: src/main/resources/db/migration/
2. Naming: V<version>__<description>.sql (e.g., V1_0__Initial_schema.sql)
3. Versions increment: V1_0, V1_1, V2_0, etc.
4. Use descriptive names
5. One logical change per migration file
6. Never modify committed migration files

### SQL Best Practices
1. Use snake_case for table and column names
2. Always add created_at and updated_at timestamps
3. Use appropriate data types (VARCHAR(255), INT, BIGINT, BOOLEAN, etc.)
4. Add NOT NULL constraints where appropriate
5. Add UNIQUE constraints for unique fields
6. Create indexes on frequently queried columns
7. Use FOREIGN KEY constraints for relationships
8. Example migration:
   ```sql
   CREATE TABLE vaccination_records (
     id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
     user_id BIGINT NOT NULL,
     vaccine_name VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
     vaccination_date DATETIME NOT NULL,
     created_at DATETIME DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
     updated_at DATETIME DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
     FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id),
     INDEX idx_user_id (user_id),
     INDEX idx_vaccination_date (vaccination_date)
   );
   ```

## Performance & Optimization

### Database Optimization
1. Use pagination for large result sets (@PageableDefault)
2. Use @Query with projections to fetch only needed columns
3. Avoid N+1 queries - use JOIN FETCH or @EntityGraph
4. Create indexes on frequently queried columns
5. Use readOnly=true for read-only transactions
6. Cache frequently accessed data with @Cacheable

### API Response Optimization
1. Use DTOs to avoid exposing entities
2. Include pagination info in list responses
3. Use compression for large responses
4. Include proper cache headers

### Caching
1. Use @Cacheable for read-heavy operations
2. Use @CachePut for cache updates
3. Use @CacheEvict for cache invalidation
4. Example:
   ```java
   @Cacheable(value = "vaccinations", key = "#id")
   public Vaccination getById(Long id) { }
   
   @CachePut(value = "vaccinations", key = "#vaccination.id")
   public Vaccination update(Vaccination vaccination) { }
   
   @CacheEvict(value = "vaccinations", key = "#id")
   public void deleteById(Long id) { }
   ```

## SE358 Requirements & Compliance

### Team Collaboration Rules
1. Tác tử không được merge - người khác phải merge
2. Mỗi PR phải ghi: Tác tử, Điều khiển, Review
3. Tác tử chỉ làm việc trên branch riêng, không access production

### PR Requirements
1. Create log file: log-tac-tu/PR-<number>.md
2. Include PR description with:
   - Tool name (e.g., Claude, Copilot, or "không")
   - Controller code (e.g., N07-B)
   - Reviewer code (e.g., N07-A)
   - Close issue: Đóng #<number>
   - Actual hours worked
   - Lines of code

### Code Documentation
1. Write JSDoc/JavaDoc for public methods
2. Document complex logic with inline comments
3. Keep comments up to date with code changes
4. Use Vietnamese in team communication, English in code/comments

### Issue Tracking
1. Create issues with user stories
2. Use acceptance criteria: Given-When-Then format
3. Estimate with three-point scale (low, likely, high)
4. Assign to team members using codes (N07-A, N07-B, etc.)
5. Don't use real names, MSSV, or emails in repo

### Git Workflow
1. Branch names: feature/xyz, bugfix/xyz, docs/xyz
2. Commit messages: conventional commits (feat:, fix:, docs:, etc.)
3. Pull request required before merge to main
4. At least one review required
5. Automated checks must pass before merge

